# Runs of 30 identical tokens, spread over at least 4 lines, shared by two or
# more crates.
#
# The floors are measured rather than chosen. At a 20-token window the
# workspace has 606 matches; at 45 it has one. Without the line spread the
# widest finding is thirteen files sharing the `impl Display` preamble, which
# is thirty tokens of Rust and no decision at all — a declaration packs its
# tokens into two lines, copied reasoning spreads them out.
#
# What this rule does NOT catch, said out loud so nobody reads a green gate as
# a clean tree:
#
#   - Twins that have already drifted. Five duration renderers were found by
#     hand precisely because they had diverged, and exact tokens cannot see
#     that. This rule catches copies before they rot, not after.
#   - Short law. `replace('-', "_")` inlined four times is six tokens, far
#     below any window that would not drown in false positives.
#   - Dense copies. The `is_ascii_alphanumeric() || c == '_' || …` character
#     class was caught between cook-cli and cook-lang, where it was written
#     across several lines, while two more copies of the same predicate sat at
#     cook-contracts/src/probe_key/mod.rs and cook-plan/src/registers.rs as
#     one-liners this rule cannot see. COOK-421 consolidated them into
#     cook_contracts::naming::is_bare_name_char, so the entry is gone -- but
#     the blind spot it illustrated is not, and this is what it looks like:
#     the rule found two of FIVE copies. A reader found two more, and a
#     reviewer found the fifth after that -- the live TOOL_NAME validator in
#     cook-lang/src/probe.rs, missed because the copy this sweep did count
#     (probe_key::is_tool_name) turned out to have no production caller.
#
# Keyed on the files involved, not on the matching tokens, so editing inside a
# known clone does not churn the baseline into a rewrite nobody reviews.

{"key": "crates/cook-cli/src/pipeline.rs == crates/cook-engine/src/run.rs", "sites": ["crates/cook-cli/src/pipeline.rs:658", "crates/cook-engine/src/run.rs:344"], "why": "Debt: the empty RunResult, constructed field by field in the crate that defines it and again in the crate that calls it. A new field gets a value in one place and a default in the other, and nothing says which is right. The type should offer the empty value it wants callers to use."}
{"key": "crates/cook-engine/src/executor.rs == crates/cook-shell/src/lib.rs", "sites": ["crates/cook-engine/src/executor.rs:142", "crates/cook-shell/src/lib.rs:141"], "why": "Debt: joining captured chunks of one stream into text, in the crate that captures them and in the crate that consumes them. cook-shell already owns the chunk type; the fold over it belongs there too, or with the chunk in cook-contracts."}
{"key": "crates/cook-lua-stdlib/src/fs_api.rs == crates/cook-register/src/dep_output_api.rs", "sites": ["crates/cook-lua-stdlib/src/fs_api.rs:173", "crates/cook-register/src/dep_output_api.rs:128"], "why": "Debt, mild: filling a Lua table from a path list, including the 1-based index convention Lua requires. Small, but it is a marshalling rule with two authors, and cook-lua-stdlib is the declared home for Lua-touching law."}
{"key": "crates/cook-plan/src/registers.rs == crates/cook-register/src/dep_output_api.rs", "sites": ["crates/cook-plan/src/registers.rs:538", "crates/cook-register/src/dep_output_api.rs:246"], "why": "Debt: resolving an alias-qualified name against an importee prefix, implemented in the planner and again in the register-phase API. This is import-namespace law, it decides which recipe a name refers to across a workspace, and a disagreement between the two would route a dependency to the wrong recipe silently."}
